"── 93_winui_shell.mst — WG4: the shell ──────────────────────────────────
 docs/ROADMAP.md's WG4 row: 'the one-window grammar stands'. The Mac's
 64_cocoaui.mst is the reference design and this is deliberately the SAME
 grammar in Windows-11 materials (docs/win_gui_design.md §2.1):

   a VIEW BAR across the top   — view-switcher buttons packed left, the live
                                 metrics cluster anchored right, Do It /
                                 Print It between them;
   a CONTENT HOST filling the  — one child per registered view, built LAZILY
   middle                        on first switch and shown/hidden thereafter
                                 (AppKit swaps tab items; Win32 has no such
                                 thing, so we own the show/hide ourselves);
   a TRANSCRIPT docked at the  — collapsible, newest line first, a log to
   bottom                        glance at rather than the output surface.

 WHAT THIS FILE MAY NOT DO, and why it is short as a result. Every rule from
 WG3 still binds and this file is the first one large enough to be tempted:

   * The door FLAGS; the DRAIN works (§2.4a). No method here is called from
     `window:message:wParam:lParam:`. `serviceCommand:code:from:` is a drain
     callee — it may talk to Win32 all it likes.
   * Controls are created at open time or in the drain, NEVER from a doit
     (WG3 Δ2, and creation itself sends messages).
   * `applyLayout` runs one `SetWindowPos` per control from the SETTLED
     client rect, and provokes no further layout (the drain holds the guard,
     so the WM_WINDOWPOSCHANGED it causes is declined at the door).

 Load order: after 92, whose four hooks this file overrides in turn. 92's
 own demo controls (a button, a status field, a two-column list) are what
 WG3's gate measures, so this file does not delete them — it takes the shell
 grammar over as a SUPERSET, and `MACVM_WINUI_WG4=off` puts WG3's window
 back exactly (the same escape hatch `MACVM_WINUI_CONTROLS=off` gives WG3)."

"── WinLayout, extended ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
 WG3's WinLayout stacks full-width bands from the top and gives one of them
 the remainder. WG4's grammar needs two things it does not have: a band
 anchored to the BOTTOM (the transcript dock, whose height is a user
 preference rather than a consequence of what is above it), and a way to
 slice one band into a row of cells (the view bar). Both stay PURE — no
 handle, no Win32, no window — which is what let WG3's layout tests run in a
 world with no database at all, and what lets WG4's do the same."
Object subclass: WinLayout [
    "No new instance variables: a class's shape is fixed once it exists, and
     that is a good rule — so a BOTTOM-anchored band is an ordinary entry in
     the same `bands` collection carrying a third element, `#bottom`.
     `add:height:`'s two-element entries stay exactly as WG3 wrote them and
     mean `#top` by omission."

    isBottomBand: b <Array> ^ <Boolean> [ ^b size > 2 and: [ (b at: 3) == #bottom ] ]

    topBands ^ <OrderedCollection> [ ^bands reject: [ :b | self isBottomBand: b ] ]
    bottomBands ^ <OrderedCollection> [ ^bands select: [ :b | self isBottomBand: b ] ]

    addBottom: aName <Symbol> height: h <Integer> [
        "A fixed band pinned to the BOTTOM edge. Bottom bands stack upward in
         the order added, so the first one added sits lowest — the same
         reading order as `add:height:`'s top-down stack."
        bands add: (Array with: aName with: h with: #bottom).
        ^self
    ]

    heightOfBottom: aName <Symbol> ^ <Integer> [
        | b |
        b := self bottomBands detect: [ :x | (x at: 1) == aName ] ifNone: [ nil ].
        ^b isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ b at: 2 ]
    ]

    setBottom: aName <Symbol> height: h <Integer> [
        "The transcript dock's collapse is a HEIGHT CHANGE, not a band that
         comes and goes: keeping the band in the list at height 0 means the
         collapsed layout is the same arithmetic as the open one, and the
         reopen restores a number rather than reconstructing a structure."
        bands do: [ :b |
            ((b at: 1) == aName and: [ self isBottomBand: b ])
                ifTrue: [ b at: 2 put: h. ^self ] ].
        ^self addBottom: aName height: h
    ]

    rectsIn: clientWidth <Integer> by: clientHeight <Integer> dpi: dpi <Integer> ^ <Dictionary> [
        "name -> #(x y width height) in PIXELS. Top bands stack down from the
         top, bottom bands stack up from the bottom, and the fill band takes
         whatever lies between the two stacks.

         Computed in DIP space and scaled ONCE — the property WG3's
         `testLayoutScalesWithDpi` pins, and the reason this recomputes
         rather than adjusting an inherited result."
        | dipW dipH out y fillHeight bottomTotal bottomY tops bots |
        dipW := clientWidth * 96 // dpi.
        dipH := clientHeight * 96 // dpi.
        out := Dictionary new.
        tops := self topBands.
        bots := self bottomBands.
        bottomTotal := 0.
        bots do: [ :b | bottomTotal := bottomTotal + (b at: 2) + margin ].
        fillHeight := dipH - (margin * (tops size + 1)) - bottomTotal.
        tops do: [ :b | (b at: 2) isNil ifFalse: [ fillHeight := fillHeight - (b at: 2) ] ].
        fillHeight < 0 ifTrue: [ fillHeight := 0 ].
        y := margin.
        tops do: [ :b |
            | h |
            h := (b at: 2) isNil ifTrue: [ fillHeight ] ifFalse: [ b at: 2 ].
            out at: (b at: 1) put: (Array
                with: margin * dpi // 96
                with: y * dpi // 96
                with: (dipW - (margin * 2)) * dpi // 96
                with: h * dpi // 96).
            y := y + h + margin ].
        bottomY := dipH - margin.
        bots do: [ :b |
            | h |
            h := b at: 2.
            bottomY := bottomY - h.
            out at: (b at: 1) put: (Array
                with: margin * dpi // 96
                with: bottomY * dpi // 96
                with: (dipW - (margin * 2)) * dpi // 96
                with: h * dpi // 96).
            bottomY := bottomY - margin ].
        ^out
    ]

    packRow: aRect <Array> left: leftWidths <Object> right: rightWidths <Object>
             gap: g <Integer> dpi: dpi <Integer> ^ <Array> [
        "Slice one band's PIXEL rect into a row: `leftWidths` packed from the
         left edge, `rightWidths` packed from the right edge (last element
         hugging the edge), every width in DIPs. Answers #(leftRects
         rightRects) — two OrderedCollections of pixel rects.

         Right-anchored cells are how the Mac's metrics cluster tracks the
         window on resize (its MinXMargin autoresizing mask); Win32 has no
         autoresizing, so the arithmetic is ours and it happens here, once,
         in the same pure object the band layout lives in."
        | x0 y h lefts rights x gapPx |
        x0 := aRect at: 1. y := aRect at: 2. h := aRect at: 4.
        gapPx := g * dpi // 96.
        lefts := OrderedCollection new.
        x := x0.
        leftWidths do: [ :w |
            | wp |
            wp := w * dpi // 96.
            lefts add: (Array with: x with: y with: wp with: h).
            x := x + wp + gapPx ].
        rights := OrderedCollection new.
        x := x0 + (aRect at: 3).
        rightWidths reverseDo: [ :w |
            | wp |
            wp := w * dpi // 96.
            x := x - wp.
            rights addFirst: (Array with: x with: y with: wp with: h).
            x := x - gapPx ].
        ^Array with: lefts with: rights
    ]
]


"── WinControl, extended ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
 WG3's control speaks text, position and messages. WG4's shell needs two
 more verbs, and both are one Win32 call each: a view's content child is
 SHOWN or HIDDEN as views switch (Win32's answer to AppKit swapping a tab
 item's view), and a command button is ENABLED or not by what the active
 view can supply."
Object subclass: WinControl [
    show: aBoolean <Boolean> ^ <Boolean> [
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi showWindow: handle
            cmd: (aBoolean
                ifTrue: [ WinApi constant: 'SW_SHOW' ]
                ifFalse: [ WinApi constant: 'SW_HIDE' ]))
    ]

    enable: aBoolean <Boolean> ^ <Boolean> [
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi enableWindow: handle enable: (aBoolean ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ]))
    ]
]

"── WinApi, one more binding ─────────────────────────────────────────────"
Object subclass: WinApi [
    WinApi class >> enableWindow: hwnd <Integer> enable: bEnable <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #EnableWindow ret: #g args: #(g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'EnableWindow'
    ]
]

"── the shell ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"
Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: Wg4Enabled Views ActiveView ViewSwitchCount ViewBuildCount
                TranscriptCollapsed TranscriptLines MetricsText MetricsUpdates
                CommandsEnabled AccentColor LabelFont GlyphFont DrawRect DrawCalls DrawMisses DrawError DrawLast DrawIds DrawResult DrawResultClass DrawStep GlyphBuf LastFocusHwnd FocusChanges TranscriptHeight DragActive DragStartY DragStartHeight MonoFont SelScratch DoItCount PrintItCount CursorScratch>

    WinShell class >> wg4Enabled ^ <Boolean> [
        "`MACVM_WINUI_WG4=off` restores WG3's window exactly — the same shape
         of escape hatch `controlsEnabled` gives WG3, and for the same
         reason: a gate that can only run the newest layer cannot bisect.
         Read on the Rust side and pushed in (win_gui/src/main.rs), exactly
         as `controlsEnabled:` is: the guest never reads the environment."
        ^Wg4Enabled ~~ false
    ]

    WinShell class >> wg4Enabled: aBoolean <Boolean> [ Wg4Enabled := aBoolean. ^self ]

    WinShell class >> requestLayout [
        "Ask the drain for a layout pass. Setting the flag is all a caller may
         do — the pass itself belongs to `drainPass`."
        PendingLayout := true.
        ^self
    ]

    "── the view registry ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A view is #(symbol title builtFlag). The Mac registers a container BLOCK
     and AppKit builds it on first tab selection; we register the same
     intent and `ensureViewBuilt:` is where laziness actually happens."

    WinShell class >> views ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        Views isNil ifTrue: [ Views := OrderedCollection new ].
        ^Views
    ]

    WinShell class >> registerViewNamed: aSymbol <Symbol> title: aTitle <String> [
        (self views anySatisfy: [ :v | (v at: 1) == aSymbol ])
            ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        self views add: (Array with: aSymbol with: aTitle with: false).
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> registerShellViews [
        "WG4 ships three views. WG5's Workspace and Browser register
         themselves from their own files, exactly as CocoaBrowser2 and the
         rest do on the Mac — which is why this is a REGISTRY and not a
         hard-coded switch statement."
        self registerViewNamed: #workspace title: 'Workspace'.
        self registerViewNamed: #welcome title: 'Welcome'.
        self registerViewNamed: #transcript title: 'Transcript'.
        self registerViewNamed: #metrics title: 'Metrics'.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> activeView ^ <Object> [ ^ActiveView ]

    WinShell class >> switchToView: aSymbol <Symbol> ^ <Boolean> [
        "Show one view's content child and hide the rest. Called from the
         DRAIN (a view-bar button's `serviceCommand:`) — never from the door.

         Answers whether the view exists, so a caller driving this from the
         control port can tell 'switched' from 'no such view' without
         parsing a transcript line."
        | entry |
        entry := self views detect: [ :v | (v at: 1) == aSymbol ] ifNone: [ nil ].
        entry isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        self ensureViewBuilt: aSymbol.
        "SHOW the incoming child FIRST, then hide the others. The obvious loop
         — one pass calling `show:` with a boolean — hides the outgoing view
         before showing the incoming one whenever the outgoing sorts first, and
         that leaves a frame in which NEITHER is on screen. A tab control never
         has that frame; it is the whole difference between a swap and a blink."
        (self controlNamed: (self contentNameFor: aSymbol))
            ifNotNil: [ :c | c show: true ].
        self views do: [ :v |
            | c |
            (v at: 1) == aSymbol ifFalse: [
                c := self controlNamed: (self contentNameFor: (v at: 1)).
                c isNil ifFalse: [ c show: false ] ] ].
        ActiveView := aSymbol.
        ViewSwitchCount := (ViewSwitchCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ ViewSwitchCount ]) + 1.
        "ONLY the bar is stale — the underline moved. Deliberately NOT a
         relayout: every content child occupies the SAME rect, so switching
         views changes no geometry at all. The earlier `requestLayout` here
         moved every control in the window on every switch, including the four
         hidden ones, which is what made a swap look like a full redraw."
        self invalidateBar.
        self syncCommandEnablement.
        ^true
    ]

    WinShell class >> viewSwitchCount ^ <Integer> [
        ^ViewSwitchCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ ViewSwitchCount ] ]

    WinShell class >> contentNameFor: aSymbol <Symbol> ^ <Symbol> [
        ^('content_' , aSymbol asString) asSymbol
    ]

    WinShell class >> viewIsBuilt: aSymbol <Symbol> ^ <Boolean> [
        | entry |
        entry := self views detect: [ :v | (v at: 1) == aSymbol ] ifNone: [ nil ].
        ^entry notNil and: [ (entry at: 3) == true ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> ensureViewBuilt: aSymbol <Symbol> [
        "LAZY BUILD — the WG4 row's own word. A view's content child is
         created the first time it is shown and never again, which is what
         makes a shell with eight views cost one view's worth of handles at
         startup. Creation sends messages; this runs in the drain, so that is
         allowed here and would not be in the door."
        | entry c |
        (self viewIsBuilt: aSymbol) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        entry := self views detect: [ :v | (v at: 1) == aSymbol ] ifNone: [ nil ].
        entry isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        "WG5a: the Workspace is EDITABLE and every other view is not — which is
         also what makes WG4 D4's focus predicate light up here and nowhere
         else, with no change to that file. A read-only EDIT is not a source
         surface; this one is."
        c := self
            addControl: (self contentNameFor: aSymbol)
            class: 'EDIT'
            text: (aSymbol == #workspace ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ self placeholderTextFor: aSymbol ])
            style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'WS_BORDER') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VSCROLL')))
                bitOr: (((WinApi constant: 'ES_MULTILINE')
                    bitOr: (aSymbol == #workspace
                        ifTrue: [ 0 ]
                        ifFalse: [ WinApi constant: 'ES_READONLY' ]))
                    bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'ES_AUTOVSCROLL')))
            exStyle: 0.
        aSymbol == #workspace ifTrue: [
            c setFont: self monoFont.
            self installGhost ].
        c show: false.
        "Position it NOW, at the content rect it will always occupy. A view
         switch deliberately does not relayout (every content child shares one
         rect, so a switch changes no geometry) — which means a child built
         after the last layout would otherwise never be given a position at
         all, and would show as an empty content area. Measured exactly that
         way the first time the relayout came out."
        self isOpen ifTrue: [
            | rects |
            rects := self layout
                rectsIn: self clientWidth
                by: self clientHeight
                dpi: (Dpi isNil ifTrue: [ WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI' ] ifFalse: [ Dpi ]).
            c moveTo: (rects at: #content) ].
        entry at: 3 put: true.
        ViewBuildCount := (ViewBuildCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ ViewBuildCount ]) + 1.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> viewBuildCount ^ <Integer> [
        ^ViewBuildCount isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ ViewBuildCount ] ]

    WinShell class >> placeholderTextFor: aSymbol <Symbol> ^ <String> [
        "Every empty state carries its teaching text (WG8's row says it for
         the whole shell; there is no reason for WG4's own views to wait)."
        aSymbol == #welcome ifTrue: [
            ^'MACVM on Windows on ARM64.', (String with: Character cr with: Character nl),
             (String with: Character cr with: Character nl),
             'This is the WG4 shell: a view bar above, a transcript docked below,',
             (String with: Character cr with: Character nl),
             'and live VM metrics on the right. Views build lazily — this one',
             (String with: Character cr with: Character nl),
             'was created the first time you looked at it.' ].
        aSymbol == #transcript ifTrue: [
            ^'The transcript view. Newest line first.' ].
        aSymbol == #metrics ifTrue: [
            ^'VM metrics, sampled off the primary.' ].
        ^''
    ]

    "── the transcript dock ──────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> transcriptOpenHeight ^ <Integer> [ ^120 ]
    WinShell class >> transcriptCollapsedHeight ^ <Integer> [ ^0 ]

    WinShell class >> transcriptCollapsed ^ <Boolean> [ ^TranscriptCollapsed == true ]

    WinShell class >> toggleTranscript ^ <Boolean> [
        "Collapse or reopen the dock. Answers the NEW collapsed state.
         A height change plus a relayout — the band never leaves the layout
         (see `setBottom:height:`), so this cannot get the arithmetic into a
         state the open path does not also produce."
        TranscriptCollapsed := self transcriptCollapsed not.
        self layout
            setBottom: #transcript
            height: (self transcriptCollapsed
                ifTrue: [ self transcriptCollapsedHeight ]
                ifFalse: [ self transcriptOpenHeight ]).
        (self controlNamed: #transcript) ifNotNil: [ :c |
            c show: self transcriptCollapsed not ].
        self requestLayout.
        ^self transcriptCollapsed
    ]

    WinShell class >> appendTranscript: aString <String> [
        "Newest line FIRST — the Mac's reverse-chronological dock, and for
         its stated reason: a log to glance at, not a stream to follow."
        | lines |
        TranscriptLines isNil ifTrue: [ TranscriptLines := OrderedCollection new ].
        TranscriptLines addFirst: aString.
        [ TranscriptLines size > 200 ] whileTrue: [ TranscriptLines removeLast ].
        (self controlNamed: #transcript) ifNotNil: [ :c |
            "A Win32 multiline EDIT breaks lines on CRLF and on nothing else:
             a bare CR renders as no break at all, which is what ran every
             transcript line together in the first WG4 snap."
            lines := String new.
            TranscriptLines do: [ :l |
                lines := lines , l , (String with: Character cr with: Character nl) ].
            c setText: lines ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> resetTranscript [
        "The tests need a known starting point, and a class-side collection
         with no reset is a test-ordering bug waiting to be blamed on
         something else."
        TranscriptLines := OrderedCollection new.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> transcriptFirstLine ^ <String> [
        ^(TranscriptLines isNil or: [ TranscriptLines isEmpty ])
            ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ TranscriptLines first ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> transcriptLineCount ^ <Integer> [
        ^TranscriptLines isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ TranscriptLines size ] ]

    "── the metrics cluster ──────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> updateMetricsMem: mem <String> jit: jit <String> code: code <String>
                       alloc: alloc <String> gc: gc <String> [
        "The Mac's own selector, same fields, same semantics — sampled off
         the PRIMARY and pushed in. Kept as ONE call because the five values
         belong to one sample: five separate setters would let the cluster
         show a mixture of two moments, which is exactly the kind of small
         lie a metrics readout must not tell."
        MetricsText := 'MEM ' , mem , '   JIT ' , jit , '   CODE ' , code ,
                       '   ALLOC ' , alloc , '   GC ' , gc.
        (self controlNamed: #metrics) ifNotNil: [ :c | c setText: MetricsText ].
        MetricsUpdates := (MetricsUpdates isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ MetricsUpdates ]) + 1.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> metricsText ^ <String> [
        ^MetricsText isNil ifTrue: [ '' ] ifFalse: [ MetricsText ] ]

    WinShell class >> metricsUpdates ^ <Integer> [
        ^MetricsUpdates isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ MetricsUpdates ] ]

    "── Do It / Print It enablement ──────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> commandsEnabled ^ <Boolean> [
        "The WG4 row: 'Do It/Print It enablement tracked by focus'. Until
         WG5's Workspace exists there is no editable text to act on, so the
         honest answer is 'enabled exactly when a view that can supply source
         is active' — today none can, and this reads false rather than
         offering a button that would do nothing."
        ^CommandsEnabled == true
    ]

    "WG4 D4 moved `syncCommandEnablement` to 95_winui_focus.mst, where the
     predicate is about the FOCUSED CONTROL rather than the active view. The
     old body tracked `ActiveView == #workspace`, which agrees with focus only
     while exactly one view can supply source — see that file's own note."

    "── the hooks 92 defined, taken over ─────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> layout ^ <WinLayout> [
        Layout isNil ifTrue: [
            Layout := self wg4Enabled ifTrue: [ self shellLayout ] ifFalse: [ WinLayout shell ] ].
        ^Layout
    ]

    WinShell class >> shellLayout ^ <WinLayout> [
        "The one-window grammar, as arithmetic: a view bar, a content area
         that fills, and a transcript docked at the bottom."
        ^WinLayout new
            add: #viewbar height: 34;
            fill: #content;
            addBottom: #transcript height: self transcriptOpenHeight;
            addBottom: #splitter height: self splitterHeight;
            yourself
    ]

    WinShell class >> buildControls ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        "WG3's controls first (its gate measures them), then the shell on
         top. Open time only — never a doit, never a handler."
        | built |
        built := self wg3BuildControls.
        self wg4Enabled ifFalse: [ ^built ].
        self controlsEnabled ifFalse: [ ^built ].
        self registerShellViews.
        ViewBuildCount := 0.
        ViewSwitchCount := 0.

        "The view bar's buttons: one per registered view, then the commands."
        self views do: [ :v |
            self
                addControl: (self viewButtonNameFor: (v at: 1))
                class: 'BUTTON'
                text: (v at: 2)
                style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                    bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VISIBLE'))
                    bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'WS_TABSTOP')
                        bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'BS_OWNERDRAW')))
                exStyle: 0 ].
        #(#doit 'Do It' #printit 'Print It') pairsDo: [ :sym :label |
            self
                addControl: sym
                class: 'BUTTON'
                text: label
                style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                    bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VISIBLE'))
                    bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'WS_TABSTOP')
                        bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'BS_OWNERDRAW')))
                exStyle: 0 ].

        "The metrics readout: right-anchored, static, monospaced digits by
         virtue of the shell font — a STATIC and not an EDIT because it is a
         readout and a caret in it would be a lie about what it accepts."
        self
            addControl: #metrics
            class: 'STATIC'
            text: 'MEM —   JIT —   CODE —   ALLOC —   GC —'
            style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VISIBLE'))
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'SS_RIGHT'))
            exStyle: 0.

        "The splitter: an owner-drawn strip the user can see and drag."
        self
            addControl: #splitter
            class: 'BUTTON'
            text: ''
            style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VISIBLE'))
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'BS_OWNERDRAW'))
            exStyle: 0.

        "The transcript dock."
        self
            addControl: #transcript
            class: 'EDIT'
            text: ''
            style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'WS_VISIBLE') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_BORDER')))
                bitOr: (((WinApi constant: 'ES_MULTILINE')
                    bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'ES_READONLY'))
                    bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'ES_AUTOVSCROLL')))
            exStyle: 0.

        "WG3's demo trio has no band in this grammar. They stay ALIVE — the
         drain, the door and their ids are unchanged — but a control with no
         rect in the active layout must not be left drawing wherever it was
         created."
        #(#button #status #list) do: [ :n |
            (self controlNamed: n) ifNotNil: [ :c | c show: false ] ].
        "§2.1: the transcript is a log OF CODE, so it wears Cascadia Mono."
        (self controlNamed: #transcript) ifNotNil: [ :c | c setFont: self monoFont ].
        self appendTranscript: 'WG4 shell up — ', self views size printString, ' views registered.'.
        self switchToView: #workspace.
        self syncCommandEnablement.
        ^Controls
    ]

    WinShell class >> viewButtonNameFor: aSymbol <Symbol> ^ <Symbol> [
        ^('view_' , aSymbol asString) asSymbol
    ]

    WinShell class >> applyLayout ^ <Integer> [
        "One SetWindowPos per control, from the settled client rect. WG3's
         controls keep their own bands; the shell's own children are placed
         from the three bands above plus one row slice for the view bar."
        | moved rects row lefts rights btnW avail verbW dipW |
        self wg4Enabled ifFalse: [ ^self wg3ApplyLayout ].
        self isOpen ifFalse: [ ^0 ].
        moved := 0.
        rects := self layout
            rectsIn: self clientWidth
            by: self clientHeight
            dpi: (Dpi isNil ifTrue: [ WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI' ] ifFalse: [ Dpi ]).

        "The view bar: view buttons and the two commands packed left, the
         metrics cluster anchored right."
        "Cell widths are NOT constants: a fourth view pushed `Print It` off the
         end of the strip at 900px, and the sixth would have taken `Do It`
         with it. The views share whatever is left after the metrics cluster
         and the two verbs, floored so a very narrow window clips rather than
         inverting."
        dipW := self clientWidth * 96 // (Dpi isNil ifTrue: [ 96 ] ifFalse: [ Dpi ]).
        verbW := 72.
        avail := dipW - 20 - 320 - (verbW * 2) - (6 * (self views size + 2)).
        btnW := (avail // (self views size max: 1)) min: 96.
        btnW := btnW max: 44.
        row := self layout
            packRow: (rects at: #viewbar)
            left: ((self views collect: [ :v | btnW ]) asArray , (Array with: verbW with: verbW))
            right: (Array with: 320)
            gap: 6
            dpi: (Dpi isNil ifTrue: [ 96 ] ifFalse: [ Dpi ]).
        lefts := row at: 1.
        rights := row at: 2.
        self views doWithIndex: [ :v :i |
            (self controlNamed: (self viewButtonNameFor: (v at: 1))) ifNotNil: [ :c |
                (c moveTo: (lefts at: i)) ifTrue: [ moved := moved + 1 ] ] ].
        (self controlNamed: #doit) ifNotNil: [ :c |
            (c moveTo: (lefts at: self views size + 1)) ifTrue: [ moved := moved + 1 ] ].
        (self controlNamed: #printit) ifNotNil: [ :c |
            (c moveTo: (lefts at: self views size + 2)) ifTrue: [ moved := moved + 1 ] ].
        (self controlNamed: #metrics) ifNotNil: [ :c |
            (c moveTo: (rights at: 1)) ifTrue: [ moved := moved + 1 ] ].

        "Every view's content child gets the SAME content rect — only the
         active one is visible, so overlapping them is not a bug but the
         mechanism (Win32's answer to AppKit swapping a tab item's view)."
        self views do: [ :v |
            (self controlNamed: (self contentNameFor: (v at: 1))) ifNotNil: [ :c |
                (c moveTo: (rects at: #content)) ifTrue: [ moved := moved + 1 ] ] ].

        (self controlNamed: #splitter) ifNotNil: [ :c |
            (c moveTo: (rects at: #splitter)) ifTrue: [ moved := moved + 1 ] ].
        (self controlNamed: #transcript) ifNotNil: [ :c |
            self transcriptCollapsed
                ifFalse: [ (c moveTo: (rects at: #transcript)) ifTrue: [ moved := moved + 1 ] ] ].

        "WG4 D3: the strip's cells are owner-drawn, and every SetWindowPos above
         happened inside this VM entry — so any paint they provoked was declined
         at the door as busy. Queue a repaint instead; it arrives through the
         pump once this pass has released the guard."
        self invalidateBar.
        ^moved
    ]

    WinShell class >> serviceCommand: anId <Integer> code: aCode <Integer> from: aHandle <Integer> [
        "A view-bar button, one drain pass after the click. Everything here
         is synchronous Win32 work — which is precisely why it is here and
         not in the door."
        | c name |
        c := self controlWithId: anId.
        c isNil ifTrue: [ ^self wg3ServiceCommand: anId code: aCode from: aHandle ].
        name := c name.
        self views do: [ :v |
            name == (self viewButtonNameFor: (v at: 1)) ifTrue: [
                self switchToView: (v at: 1).
                self appendTranscript: 'switched to ' , (v at: 2).
                ^self ] ].
        "WG5a landed the Workspace, so these do the thing rather than explain
         that they cannot. Reported by the author as unresponsive to a click —
         the buttons were live and drawing, and this handler was still WG4's
         placeholder, which is the least visible kind of stale code: it
         RESPONDS, so nothing looks broken until you read what it says."
        name == #doit ifTrue: [ self doIt. ^self ].
        name == #printit ifTrue: [ self printIt. ^self ].
        name == #splitter ifTrue: [ self onSplitterClick. ^self ].
        ^self wg3ServiceCommand: anId code: aCode from: aHandle
    ]
]
